ps://link.mail.beehiiv.\\\"><p style=\\\"font-size:14px;color:inherit;\\\"> Already gone </p></a></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td></tr><tr><td align=\\\"center\\\" valign=\\\"top\\\" style=\\\"font-size:0px;line-height:0px;padding:16px 0px 12px;\\\" class=\\\"dd\\\"><table class=\\\"j\\\" role=\\\"none\\\" width=\\\"100%\\\" border=\\\"0\\\" cellspacing=\\\"0\\\" cellpadding=\\\"0\\\" align=\\\"center\\\"><tbody><tr><td> &nbsp; </td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr><td class=\\\"dd\\\" align=\\\"left\\\" style=\\\"padding:0px 0px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;\\\"><p style=\\\"\\\"> When I interviewed Tibo from OpenAI recently, I asked him if product, design, engineering and product management are all turning into one role. </p></td></tr><tr><td class=\\\"dd\\\" align=\\\"left\\\" style=\\\"padding:0px 0px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;\\\"><p style=\\\"\\\"> He smiled and said &quot;<i>people with great taste are having a great time,</i>&quot; which I took to mean that once one person can do four jobs with AI, the hard part becomes knowing which output is right. </p></td></tr><tr><td class=\\\"dd\\\" align=\\\"left\\\" style=\\\"padding:0px 0px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;\\\"><p style=\\\"\\\"> I kept thinking about the flip side of that all week. If AI is doing the work, who&#39;s on the hook when it gets something wrong? </p></td></tr><tr><td class=\\\"dd\\\" align=\\\"left\\\" style=\\\"padding:0px 0px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;\\\"><p style=\\\"\\\"> If you missed the Tibo episode, <b>it&#39;s here:</b></p></td></tr><tr><td class=\\\"dd\\\" align=\\\"center\\\" valign=\\\"top\\\" style=\\\"padding:20px;\\\"><a href=\\\"https://link.mail.beehiiv.\\\" style=\\\"text-decoration:none;\\\"><table align=\\\"center\\\" width=\\\"100%\\\" cellpadding=\\\"0\\\" cellspacing=\\\"0\\\" border=\\\"0\\\" role=\\\"none\\\" style=\\\"max-width:520px;margin:0 auto;\\\"><tbody><tr><td class=\\\"p\\\" width=\\\"100%\\\" style=\\\"paddi