('oa0ui38n7i','2026-10-17 16:35:42.493558','2026-10-17 16:35:42.493558','Administrator','Administrator',0,0,'aak8vjhnlh','Communication',0,'','{\n \"_user_tags\": null,\n \"bcc\": null,\n \"cc\": null,\n \"comment_type\": \"\",\n \"communication_date\": \"2026-09-25 14:40:19\",\n \"communication_medium\": \"Email\",\n \"communication_type\": \"Communication\",\n \"content\": \" <meta charset=\\\"UTF-8\\\"> <meta content=\\\"IE=edge\\\"> <meta name=\\\"viewport\\\" content=\\\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\\\"> <title>Emailer</title>\\n<center style=\\\"width:600px; margin:10px auto;\\\"> <img border=\\\"0\\\" src=\\\"https://lnk.info.bigbasket.com/vtrack?clientid=18595&amp;ul=\\nUlANUVQBBVdFB0lGUllXUUBMRwpZAFpEXgdYQwxWUQdYIV4OA1peTFpZWEhBUV9RWEBBEVkNXFNFC1ReEApTBVNYeQQPUlsOF1VaWU9MB0wI&amp;ml=V1YIW1IDAx4JSgVI&amp;sl=Ix12HzdgTjF0YklYXVsYWVdfW0xTDF5SUBdcVBZKAloMHQkfUk8C&amp;c=0000\\\"> <!-- Header --> <div class=\\\"container\\\" style=\\\"width:100%; border:1px solid lightgrey;\\\"> <section class=\\\"header\\\"> <div style=\\\"clear:both;\\\"> <img src=\\\"https://blog.bigbasket.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/header_24Sep_2026.jpg\\\" style=\\\"width:100%; display:block; margin:0px 0px;\\\">\\n<h1 style=\\\"text-align:center; font-size:24px;\\\"> Powered by <i>Chutney</i> </h1> <p style=\\\"text-align:center; padding:0px 40px 20px 40px; font-size: 18px; line-height:32px;\\\"> True to its root word <i>ch&#257;&#7789;n&#257;</i>\\n(\\\"to lick\\\"), a great <i>chutney</i> leaves you chasing every last drop on the plate. Whether pounded with sea salt into a fiery <i>thecha</i> or sizzled in hot mustard oil, this little dollop gives every meal its pulse. This World <i>\\nChutney</i> Day, we\'re venturing beyond the usual staples to explore rare regional recipes and global spin-offs. Find your next <i>chutney</i> obsession here! </p></div> </section> <!-- Blog-1 --> <section class=\\\"blog1\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom:70px;\\\"> <div style=\\\"clear:both;\\\">\\n<div style=\\\"padding:0px 20px;\\\"> <img src=\\\"https://blog.bigbasket.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/blog01-1_24Sep_2026.jpg\\\" style=\\\"width:100%; display:block; border-radius: 15px;\\\"> </div> <div style=\\\"clear: both; padding: 0px 0px 0px 140px; height: 100px; width: 549px;\\\"> <div style=\\\"float:left;\\\">\\n<span style=\\\"font-size: 70px; color:#b5d168; font-family: Arial Black,Arial Bold,Gadget,sans-serif;\\\"> <b>#</b> </span> <span style=\\\"font-size: 115px; color:#b5d168; font-family: Arial Black,Arial Bold,Gadget,sans-serif; margin-right:15px;\\\"> <b>\\n1</b> </span> </div> <div style=\\\"float:left; padding-top:40px; text-align:left;padding-top: 58px;\\\"> <span style=\\\"font-weight: bold; font-size: 22px; text-align:left;\\\"> India&rsquo;s Most <br> Underrated <i>\\nChutneys</i> </span> </div> </div> <div style=\\\"clear:both;\\\"></div> <p style=\\\"text-align:center; margin:0px 0px 20px 0px; padding:0px 40px; font-size: 18px; line-height:32px;\\\"> Mint-coriander and sweet-tamarind will always hold a special place in our hearts, but India&rsquo;s mortar and pestle traditions run much deeper. From the zesty bite of tomato and <i>\\nkasundi</i> to peppery drumstick leaves, regional kitchens prove that simple ingredients, when crushed with love, can turn into something truly special. Discover four lesser-known <i>chutney</i> recipes you can easily make at home.</p>\\n<a href=\\\"https://lnk.info.bigbasket.com/vtrack?clientid=18595&amp;ul=\\nUlANUVQBBVdFB0lGUllXUUBMRwpZAFpEXgdYQwxWUQdYIV4OA1peTFpZWEhBUV9RWEBBEVkNXFNFC1ReEApTBVNYeQQPUlsOF1VaWU9MB0wI&amp;ml=V1YIW1IDAx4JSgQDCgQATA==&amp;sl=Ix16HzdgTjF0YklYXVsYWVdfW0xTDF5SUBdcVBZKAloMHQkfUk8C&amp;pp=0&amp;fl=CRVNExEJHU1bWlpTHVJfV1tYRwlUERdTXgkY&amp;ext=cD0xNzU1ODE=\\\"> <!--The blog 1 link should be here--><img src=\\\"https://www.bigbasket.com/media/uploads/flatpages/mailer-images-aug/cookbook-mailer-readmore-button.gif\\\" style=\\\"width:25%; display:block;\\\"> </a>\\n</div> </section> <!-- Blog-2 --> <section class=\\\"blog2\\\" style=\\\"margin-bottom:70px;\\\"> <div style=\\\"clear:both;\\\"> <div style=\\\"padding:0px 20px;\\\"> <img src=\\\"https://blog.bigbasket.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/blog02-1_24Sep_2026.jpg\\\" style=\\\"width:100%; display:block; border-radius: 15px;\\\"> </div>\\n<div style=\\\"clear: both; padding: 0px 0px 0px 140px; height: 100px; width: 549px;\\\"> <div style=\\\"float:left;\\\"> <span style=\\\"font-size: 70px; color:#b5d168; font-family: Arial Black,Arial Bold,Gadget,sans-serif;\\\"> <b>\\n#</b> </span> <span style=\\\"font-size: 115px; color:#b5d168; font-family: Arial Black,Arial Bold,Gadget,sans-serif; margin-right:15px;\\\"> <b>2</b> </span> </div> <div style=\\\"float:left; padding-top:40px; text-align:left;padding-top: 58px;\\\"> <span style=\\\"font-weight: bold; font-size: 22px; text-align:left;\\\">\\n<i>Chutney</i> <br> Goes Global </span> </div> </div> <div style=\\\"clear:both;\\\"></div> <p style=\\\"text-align:center; margin:0px 0px 20px 0px; padding:0px 40px; font-size: 18px; line-height:32px;\\\"> What do a British cheese board, a Swedish sandwich, and a South African spread have in common? A love for <i>\\nchutney</i>! Born in India and adapted across continents, <i>chutney</i> evolved from fresh, punchy blends into tangy, slow-cooked preserves made to travel and last. From rich British apple and onion <i>\\nchutney</i> to warmly spiced South African <i>blatjang</i>&mdash;a thick, sweet-and-spicy apricot <i>chutney</i>&mdash;these recipes explore how each version balances sweetness, acidity, and spice. </p> <a href=\\\"https://lnk.info.bigbasket.com/vtrack?clientid=18595&amp;ul=\\nUlANUVQBBVdFB0lGUllXUUBMRwpZAFpEXgdYQwxWUQdYIV4OA1peTFpZWEhBUV9RWEBBEVkNXFNFC1ReEApTBVNYeQQPUlsOF1VaWU9MB0wI&amp;ml=V1YIW1IDAx4JSgQDCgQBTA==&amp;sl=Ix16HzdgTjF0YklYXVsYWVdfW0xTDF5SUBdcVBZKAloMHQkfUk8C&amp;pp=0&amp;fl=CRVNExEJHU1bWlpTHVJfV1tYRwlUERdTXgkY&amp;ext=cD0xNzU1ODM=\\\">\\n<!--The blog 2 link should be here-->